  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Saturday)\nIssued at 1217 AM CDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nNo major changes to note in the extended period. Headlines of \nimpactful and consecutive heat continue as the upper ridge remains \nstrong aloft. Long range models are showing a bit more consistency \nin the eventual westward movement of the H500 high. However, \ntemperatures are not currently expected to experience a substantial \nshift for the first week of August. Stronger upper level forcing is \nrequired to suppress the ridge, and guidance does not show this \noccurring any time soon.\n\nIn the meantime, hot +100 degree highs continue across much of the \nCWA for the next 7 days. The hottest days of the week look to occur \nat the forefront due to the position of the high pressure center \nbeing almost directly overhead. Temperatures are forecast to undergo \na small downtrend towards the end of the week. High could \npotentially fall below 100 for many locations on Saturday, as long \nas temperature trends hold. If the upper high takes longer to \ntransition west, triple digit heat could persist well into August.\n\nMoisture continues to be somewhat lacking in the long term period. \nLike previous forecasts, northwest flow could eventually allow \nprecipitation to move into the northwest combined Panhandles as \nconvection transitions off the high terrain. PoPs are still limited \nunder this regime, as the placement of the upper high will influence \nthe coverage of showers and storms in our area.\n\nRangel",
